# .gitleaksignore — reviewed false positives for the `security-scan` CI job
#
# ─── Why this file exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `security-scan` (.gitlab-ci.yml) runs `gitleaks detect --source . --exit-code 1`
# over the whole of git history. It was red on every run, and no submitted
# document mentioned it. A permanently-red secret scanner is worse than no
# scanner: nobody reads a job that is always red, so the run that finds a REAL
# credential looks exactly like the 26 runs before it.
#
# This file makes the job green **honestly** — by recording that each finding was
# opened and judged, not by muting the rule or skipping the job.
#
# ─── What was measured, and how ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Scanned 2026-08-15 at `pf/integration` HEAD (dbfb46d), with BOTH the version CI
# pins and a newer one, because gitleaks' default rule set changes between
# releases and a scan that only matches one of them proves nothing:
#
#   gitleaks 8.28.0 (CI's pin, .gitlab-ci.yml)  → 26 findings
#   gitleaks 8.30.1                             → 26 findings, identical
#   the same scan limited to `origin/main`      → 20 findings, a strict subset
#
# All 26 are `generic-api-key`. Every one was opened and read in context. There
# is **no real credential** among them, and every entry below says why in its own
# words rather than deferring to a blanket "test fixture" label.
#
# ─── The specific check that mattered ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `/ship/dev/WEBHOOK_SECRET_KEY` was provisioned in SSM on 2026-08-15, so the
# question "did any of it reach git?" needed a direct answer rather than an
# inference from the findings list. Every value ever committed under that name
# was enumerated (`git log --all -p -S WEBHOOK_SECRET_KEY`) and each is one of:
#
#   • the literal dummy `0123456789abcdef` × 4  (CI, and a test default)
#   • `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('base64')` — generated at CI runtime
#   • `Buffer.alloc(32, 0x01)` / `Buffer.alloc(32, 0x5a)` — constant test buffers
#   • terraform's `random_password`, which writes to SSM and is never rendered
#
# The real key is generated into SSM by terraform and read at boot through
# `getSSMSecret('/ship/<env>/WEBHOOK_SECRET_KEY')`. It has never had a value in
# the repository. Also checked across all history and clean: no `AKIA…` AWS
# access key, and no `BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY` block carrying actual key material
# (`docs/fpki-auth-client-dcr-analysis.md` has PEM *headers* inside illustrative
# JSON — `"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n..."` and one truncated PKCS#8 prefix
# that is byte-identical for every RSA key ever generated; no key bytes).
#
# ─── Maintaining this file ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Entries are `commit:path:rule:line`, so they pin a finding to the commit that
# introduced it. Editing one of these files LATER creates a new fingerprint and
# the job goes red again — which is the intended behaviour. A new red line means
# a human looks at a new value. Do not add an entry without reading the matched
# value first, and do not convert this file into a path allowlist: "we reviewed
# these 26 exact strings" is an auditable claim; "ignore this directory" is not.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


